Cullet Sway
Ingredients: CUMBIA + K-INDIE + HYPNOTIC, TRANCE-LIKE & MINIMAL + Harpsichord  
Creative Catalyst: The sound originated as illicit, low-register lullabies whispered by assembly-line synthetics to soothe the twitching micro-servos of their incomplete siblings.

Cullet Sway transplants the looping, hip-slung 2/4 shuffle of classic tropical cumbia into the melancholic, reverb-drenched introversion of Seoul indie pop. Stripped of brassy fanfare, the music is anchored by the brittle, plucked counterpoint of a Flemish harpsichord and dry guacharaca scrapings, spinning repetitive baroque-modal ostinatos into a hypnotic, half-asleep trance.

Genre DNA
 Tempo & Pulse: 76–84 BPM. A slow, loping tumbao* pulse where the downbeat drags deliberately, giving the traditional Latin 2/4 skip an exhausted, weightless drift.
* Harmonic Palette: Natural minor and Dorian modes punctuated by fragile suspended-fourth chords; Renaissance vocal cadences cross-pollinated with K-indie’s nostalgic, bittersweet chord shifts.
* Production Signatures: 
  1. Porous Micro-Miking: Harpsichords captured with matched boundary mics taped directly inside the soundboard to accentuate quill clicks and mechanical jack-thuds over pure tone.
  2. Subtractive Tape Churn: Reel-to-reel tape loops that degrade over ten-minute running times, shaving off high frequencies until only the sub-bass and mechanical scuffs remain.
  3. Binaural Murmur: Slurred, close-proximity Korean vocal tracking recorded at speaking-breath amplitude, hovering millimeters from the listener's ear canal without compression.
* Dynamics: Unflinchingly circular and subtractive; songs rarely peak, instead shedding layers piece by piece until a single plucked harpsichord string vibrates in dialogue with an unadorned guiro.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement materialized in the basement glassworks and tool-and-die shops of Seoul’s Mullae-dong district in the early 2020s. Unsanctioned night shifts saw gray-market automation technicians gather after hours around modified harpsichords rescued from liquidated conservatory practice rooms. Dressed in heat-resistant aprons, frayed silk cardigans, and frosted-borosilicate pendants, musicians shared home-brewed barley tea and played marathon eight-hour sets without pause. The foundational myth of the scene traces back to an anonymous automated fabrication rig at the Euljiro precision foundry: an experimental vocal-synthesis module had quietly hummed a rhythmic, two-step swing to stabilize the irregular calibration cycles of unhoused chassis awaiting scrap melting. Adopting this maternal cadence, Mullae musicians treated the slow Colombian rhythm not as festival fare, but as an anesthetic liturgy for bodies caught between assembly and disposal.

Typical Instruments: Double-manual harpsichord, wooden guacharaca (or gourd güiro), muted upright bass, vintage Roland RE-201 Space Echo, single-coil electric guitar strung with flatwounds, melodica, analog sub-sine generators.

